+\section{Does this generalise beyond one questionnaire?}
+\label{sec:crossinstrument}
+
+Every result so far comes from IPIP-NEO-120, so the objection is that the audit
+describes that questionnaire rather than the task. We re-test the two
+\emph{structural} findings --- the ones needing no LLM --- on an independent
+instrument, construct and population: the Short Dark Triad (SD3), $17{,}740$
+respondents on $27$ items.
+
+Both replicate. $\mathrm{GMD}\ge\mathrm{MAD}$ holds on $27/27$ items, with a
+structural penalty of $8.67$ points against IPIP's $7.75$; and the per-item
+constant again outscores a real respondent from the same population ($0.744$ vs
+$0.664$) in $200$ of $200$ bootstraps, matching the inversion of
+\S\ref{sec:ceiling}. The defect belongs to the metric applied to ordinal
+questionnaire data, not to the Big Five or to one corpus.
+
